# LoRaWAN compliance — US915 Class A

Airtime is a shared resource and TTN is free infrastructure someone else pays for. A
misbehaving node degrades every other node on the gateway. Treat these as hard limits,
not targets. Citation keys in brackets resolve in `docs/CITATIONS.md`.

## Fixed parameters — do not change without an ADR

| Parameter | Value | Source |
|---|---|---|
| Region | US915 | `FIRMWARE_SPEC.md` §3 |
| Class | **A only** | §3, §8 — Class C is an explicit non-goal |
| Activation | OTAA | §3 |
| ADR | ON | §3 |
| Confirmed uplink | **OFF** | §3 |
| Default interval | 3600 s | §4 |
| Allowed interval | 1800–86400 s inclusive | §4 |
| Bench cadence | 60 s, USB-only builds, `FEATURE_RADIO=0` | §4 |

Class C is a non-goal because it holds the receiver open, which is incompatible with the
months-unattended power budget (rule 50).

## Class A is why the node is reachable only after it talks

A Class A device opens **exactly two short receive windows, RX1 and RX2, after each
uplink** [CIT-LW-LINK]. There is no other time the network can reach it. Three
consequences the firmware must respect:

1. **A downlink cannot arrive on demand.** An interval change lands on the next uplink at
   the earliest, which at the 86400 s maximum is up to a day later. Never write logic
   that waits on a downlink.
2. **The RX windows must not be missed.** Do not sleep, reconfigure RS-485, or start a
   Modbus transaction between TX and the close of RX2. Poll sensors *before* the uplink —
   this is why `FIRMWARE_SPEC.md` §5 orders the cycle poll → TX → RX → sleep.
3. **Downlink is not acknowledgement.** Silence is the normal case, not an error.

## Airtime budget — the constraint that sets the interval floor

TTN Fair Use allows roughly **30 seconds of uplink airtime per device per day** and
**about 10 downlinks per device per day** [CIT-TTN-FUP]. This is what sets the interval
floor, and it is why `FIRMWARE_SPEC.md` §4 raised that floor from 300 s to **1800 s**: an
11-byte uplink at the slowest US915 data rate takes about 370 ms, so 300 s intervals would
be 288 uplinks and roughly 107 seconds of airtime — over three times the allowance. At
1800 s it stays under 18 seconds even at the slowest rate.

Design against the slowest rate, not the typical one. ADR puts the nodes with the weakest
coverage on it, and that is exactly this deployment.

The 60 s bench cadence (`FEATURE_BENCH_INTERVAL`) is compile-time and confined to builds
with `FEATURE_RADIO=0`, so it never reaches the air. Do not combine it with the radio: at
60 s that is ~1440 uplinks/day, roughly fifty times the allowance.

Therefore:

- **Keep the payload small.** The US915 application payload limit is smallest at the
  lowest data rate [CIT-LORA-RP002], and ADR will not always leave you at a high one. A
  payload that only fits at a high DR is a payload that silently fails at the edge of
  coverage.

On TTN, US902-928 uplinks run **SF7BW125 to SF10BW125** on 903.9–905.3 MHz (plus 904.6
SF8BW500), and **RX2 is 923.3 MHz at SF12BW500** [CIT-TTN-FREQ]. Note the uplink floor is
SF10, not SF12 — do not carry EU-band intuitions across, because airtime-per-message and
the resulting messages-per-day differ.
- **Never enable confirmed uplinks.** Each one forces a downlink, drawing against the
  ~10/day downlink budget and consuming gateway TX time.
- **Never retry a failed uplink immediately.** Bounded exponential backoff only
  (`FIRMWARE_SPEC.md` H4). The node must survive multi-day gateway outages without an
  airtime death spiral — the failure mode this project exists to avoid.
- **Join must back off, and must sleep between attempts.** A tight join loop is the most
  damaging thing a node can do to a shared gateway (§3, H4) — and on this hardware it also
  kills the battery, because a node stuck retrying a join **never sleeps at all**
  [CIT-RAK-SLEEP]. See rule 50; this is one requirement, not two.
- Under US915 frequency hopping, FCC limits dwell time per channel [CIT-FCC-15247]; the
  stack enforces this, but do not defeat it by pinning a single channel.

## Downlink handling — validate, then persist

Downlinks are opcode-led on **fPort 10** (`FIRMWARE_SPEC.md` §4): a uint8 `opcode` followed
by its arguments. `0x01` set-interval carries `interval_s` as a **uint32 big-endian**, 5
bytes total; `0x03` request-status carries no arguments, 1 byte. A bare integer was rejected
because its meaning would have to be guessed from its length, and a node already in the
woods cannot be taught a new message format. Required behavior:

1. Check the fPort. Ignore other ports.
2. Check the opcode, then the length for that opcode. Wrong length → ignore.
3. Range-check **1800–86400 inclusive**. Out of range → ignore and keep the previous value.
4. Persist to flash so it survives power loss (H5), then apply on the **next** wake (§4).
5. **Never** let a downlink drive the interval outside the allowed range, and never let a
   malformed downlink reset it to a default. Invalid input keeps the previous value — an
   unreachable node with a bad interval is a hike-in recovery.

Flash wear matters: only write when the value actually changed, and never write during a
brownout (H3).

## Uplink content

Nulls stay null. A failed Modbus read omits the field or encodes the null sentinel — it
never encodes `0` (`AGENTS.md`, `FIRMWARE_SPEC.md` §2.1). A zero wind speed and a dead
anemometer must never be indistinguishable downstream.

One sensor failing does not suppress the uplink; send what was read, with validity flags
(§5). Payload channel and type IDs are governed by rule 60 — they are not free choices.
